<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  NOTE: a doubled hyphen is ILLEGAL inside an XML comment, and this file had
  three of them. Apple's own parser is lenient, so launchd loaded the plist and
  `plutil -p` printed it happily, while every strict reader rejected it and
  `ops/doctor.py` reported the plist as unreadable. Rules here are `===` and
  dashes are em dashes. Do not reinstate ASCII rules in this block.

  Nightly archive of PROD STATE. Rendered by ops/schedule/install_backup.sh.

  WHY THIS JOB DOES NOT GO THROUGH launch_job.py
  ==============================================
  Every trading job (cef, phase0, benchmarks, collect, watchdog, weekly) is
  dispatched by ~/Library/Application Support/quantt/launch_job.py, which owns
  the dev/prod boundary in its REPO constant. This job deliberately does not.
  Two reasons:

    1. launch_job.py sets up a SESSION — env file, calendar gate, heartbeat,
       halt check. A tar of a state directory needs none of that, and every
       line of session machinery it borrowed would be a line that could fail
       and take the backup with it.
    2. The backup must keep working when the session machinery is what broke.
       That is the case it exists for.

  ops/backup_state.sh derives the tree it archives from its OWN location
  ($(dirname $0)/..), so pointing this plist at the copy inside ~/prod/QUANTT
  is what makes it back up prod. Point it at the dev copy and it archives dev.
  That is intentional and is the only knob.

  TIMING: 23:55 local, every day.
  The cef session starts 17:15 and blocks on the sponsor NAV publish, whose
  hard deadline is 23:30. Observed finishes: 21:46 (2026-09-09) and 22:54
  (2026-09-08, which spent ~10 min capturing 1,529 executions). Worst realistic
  finish is therefore ~23:40, so 23:55 clears it with margin and still lands the
  archive on the trading date. A session that somehow overran would give one
  torn archive out of the 90 retained — acceptable; missing the day's fills
  entirely would not be.
  Runs all seven days, not just weekdays: state can change on a weekend by hand,
  and an archive of an unchanged tree costs 164 KB.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
	</dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.quantt.backup.daily</string>
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/bash</string>
		<string>__PROD__/ops/backup_state.sh</string>
	</array>
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>__SUPPORT__/launchd_backup.log</string>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>__SUPPORT__/launchd_backup.log</string>
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>Hour</key>
			<integer>__HOUR__</integer>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>__MINUTE__</integer>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
